    "evidence_text": "DBLP:conf/nips/KirosZSZUTF15,DBLP:conf/emnlp/ConneauKSBB17,DBLP:conf/iclr/LogeswaranL18,DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1903-09424.\n\nSkip-Thought  consists of an encoder and two decoders.\n\nWhen a sentence is given and encoded into a vector by the encoder, the decoders are trained to predict the next sentence and the previous sentence.\n\nConsidering that the estimation of the likelihood of sequences is computationally expensive and time-consuming, the Quick-Thought method  simplifies this in a manner similar to sampled softmax , which classifies the input sentences between surrounding sentences and the other.\n\nInferSent  instead pre-trains the model in a manner of supervised learning.\n\npre-training task to learn the sentence representation.\n\nMethod\tOur method follows the same input format and the model architecture with original BERT.\n\nThe NSP task is a binary classification task, which takes two sentences (A and B) as input and determines whether B is the next sentence of A. Although it has been proven to be very effective for BERT, there are two major deficiencies.\n\n(1) Discrimination between IsNext and DiffDoc (the label of the sentences drawn from different documents via negative sampling) is semantically shallow as the signal of sentence order is absent.\n\nAs reported , the final pre-trained model is able to achieve 97%-98% accuracy on the NSP task.\n\nMethod ::: Gathering More Document-level Information\tBeyond NSP and PSP, which enable the model to learn the short-term dependency between sentences, we also propose to expand the scope of discrimination task to further incorporate the document-level information.\n\nNote that these in-adjacent sentences will introduce much more training noise to the model.\n\nIt achieves this by relaxing our confidence on the labels, e.g., transforming the target probability from (1.0,\n\n0.0) to (0.8, 0.2) in a binary classification problem.\n\nIn summary, when A is given, the pre-training example for each label is constructed as follows: IsNext: Choosing the adjacent following sentence as B. IsPrev: Choosing the adjacent previous sentence as B. IsNextInadj: Choosing the in-adjacent following sentence as B. There is a sentence between A and B. IsPrevInadj: Choosing the in-adjacent previous sentence as B. There is a sentence between A and B. DiffDoc: Drawing B randomly from a different document.\n\nTo accelerate the training speed, two-phase training  is adopted.\n\nThe numbers of training steps of two phases are 50K and 40K for the BERTBase model.\n\nWe used AdamW  optimizer with a learning rate of 1e-4, a $\\beta _1$ of\n\n0.9, a $\\beta _2$ of 0.999 and a L2 weight decay rate of $0.01$.\n\nThe first 10% of the total steps are used for learning rate warming up, followed by the linear decay schema.\n\nWe used a dropout probability of 0.1 on all layers.\n\nThe data used for pre-training is the same as BERT, i.e., English Wikipedia (2500M words) and BookCorpus (800M words) .\n\nBERT-PN: The NSP task in BERT is replaced by a 3-class task with IsNext, IsPrev and DiffDoc.\n\nBERT-PNsmth: It uses the same data with BERT-PN5cls, except that the IsPrevInadj\n\n(IsNextInadj) label is mapped to IsPrev (IsNext) with a label smoothing factor of 0.8.\n\nBERT-PNsmth, which is the label-smoothed version of BERT-PN5cls, is used to compare with BERT-PN5cls to see whether the noise reduction is necessary.\n\nThe order of the input pair of NLI samples is reversed in the fine-tuning phase, and other hyper-parameters and settings keep the same with the BERT paper.\n\nwas conducted for 3 epochs for all the tasks, with a learning rate of 2e-5.\n\nThe predictions were obtained by evaluating the training checkpoint with the best validation performance.\n\nThe model computes for a concatenation a scalar as the score, which is then used in a softmax layer for the final prediction.\n\nFor Chinese NLP tasks, we pre-train the model using Chinese corpus.\n\nFor the first 3 Chinese tasks, we follow the settings as in ERNIE .\n\nThe proposed method is compared with four models, i.e., BERTBase , BERTBase with whole word masking , ERNIE  and ERNIE 2.0 .\n\nNote that the Chinese ERNIE 2.0 is equipped with 5 different\n\nIn the future, we will investigate the way to take advantages of both large-scale training and our method.",
